---
import Base from '../../../../layouts/Base.astro';

const SITE = 'https://precisionsolutionstech.com';
const title = 'Passport Photo Coach Support | Precision Solutions Tech';
const description =
  'Support for Passport Photo Coach on iPhone: taking a photo that passes, glasses, babies, printing at a drugstore or at home, digital uploads, tips, and common questions.';

const jsonLd = [
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
    itemListElement: [
      { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'iOS Apps', item: `${SITE}/ios-apps/` },
      { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: 'Passport Photo Coach', item: `${SITE}/ios-apps/passport-photo-coach/` },
      { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 3, name: 'Support', item: `${SITE}/ios-apps/passport-photo-coach/support/` },
    ],
  },
];
---

<Base title={title} description={description} path="/ios-apps/passport-photo-coach/support/" jsonLd={jsonLd} active="ios-apps">
  <div class="wrap">
    <nav class="breadcrumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
      <ol>
        <li><a href="/">Home</a></li>
        <li><a href="/ios-apps/">iOS Apps</a></li>
        <li><a href="/ios-apps/passport-photo-coach/">Passport Photo Coach</a></li>
        <li>Support</li>
      </ol>
    </nav>

    <article class="prose">
      <h1>Passport Photo Coach Support</h1>
      <p class="lead">
        Something not working, or a question the app doesn’t answer? Email
        <a href="mailto:support@precisionsolutionstech.com">support@precisionsolutionstech.com</a>
        and tell us your iPhone model, iOS version, which document you chose, and what you expected to
        happen. We read every message.
      </p>

      <h2 id="requirements">Requirements</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>iPhone with iOS 17 or later</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 id="good-photo">Taking a photo that passes</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Stand about 4 ft (1.2 m) in front of a plain, light-coloured wall</li>
        <li>Face a window or a lamp so light falls evenly on your face</li>
        <li>Take off hats, caps and headphones. Religious or medical head coverings are allowed (some documents ask for a signed statement)</li>
        <li>Follow the spoken or on-screen prompts until the oval turns green</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        The sharpest result comes from the back camera: choose <strong>Someone’s helping me</strong>, or
        <strong>Phone on a stand</strong> to be talked into position and photographed automatically.
      </p>

      <h2 id="glasses">Glasses</h2>
      <p>
        Many documents, including US passports and visas, don’t allow glasses. Keep yours on while you
        line up. When everything else is right, the app asks out loud for them to come off, counts down,
        takes the photo, and tells you when to put them back on. For headshots and badges, glasses are
        fine.
      </p>

      <h2 id="baby">Babies and young children</h2>
      <p>
        Turn on <strong>It’s for a baby or young child</strong>. Lay the baby on a plain white sheet and
        hold the phone directly above, or seat them in a car seat covered with a white sheet. Keep hands
        and toys out of the photo. The app takes the photo the moment they’re still and framed, so try a
        few.
      </p>

      <h2 id="store-print">Printing at a drugstore</h2>
      <p>
        Tap <strong>Print at CVS, Walgreens or Walmart</strong>. The app saves a 4 × 6 image with your
        photos and cut marks to Photos. Upload it to any store’s photo service and order one 4 × 6
        glossy print, usually for same-day pickup. Don’t crop, zoom or “enhance” it, or the size will be
        wrong. Then cut on the marks.
      </p>

      <h2 id="home-print">Printing at home</h2>
      <p>
        Choose your paper (4 × 6, 5 × 7, Letter or A4) and tap <strong>Print on my printer</strong>.
        Use glossy photo paper, and if your printer offers “scale to fit”, turn it off so the photo
        prints at its exact size.
      </p>

      <h2 id="digital">Online applications</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>Save the digital photo</strong> makes a JPEG at the size and file size the upload asks
        for, for example 600 × 600 pixels and under 240 KB for the DV Lottery and US visa.
      </p>

      <h2 id="tips">Tips</h2>
      <p>
        Everything in the app is free. Tips are optional and unlock nothing. Tips paid in the app are
        handled by Apple; refunds at
        <a href="https://reportaproblem.apple.com" rel="noopener">reportaproblem.apple.com</a>.
      </p>

      <h2 id="questions">Common questions</h2>
      <h3>Will my photo be accepted?</h3>
      <p>
        The app checks your photo against each document’s published rules, but the checks are automatic
        and the agency always decides. The review screen links to the official rules for your document.
      </p>
      <h3>The checklist says “Couldn’t check this automatically”.</h3>
      <p>
        That item couldn’t be measured in your photo. Look at it yourself, or retake the photo in better
        light.
      </p>
      <h3>My document isn’t listed.</h3>
      <p>
        Email us with the country and document and a link to its photo rules. We add documents people
        ask for.
      </p>
      <h3>Does the app upload my photo?</h3>
      <p>
        No. Everything runs on your iPhone. See the
        <a href="/legal/privacy/passport-photo-coach-ios/">privacy policy</a>.
      </p>

      <h2 id="contact">Contact</h2>
      <p>
        <a href="mailto:support@precisionsolutionstech.com">support@precisionsolutionstech.com</a>
      </p>
      <p>
        <a href="/ios-apps/passport-photo-coach/">Passport Photo Coach</a>
        ·
        <a href="/legal/privacy/passport-photo-coach-ios/">Privacy Policy</a>
        ·
        <a href="/about/">About us</a>
      </p>
    </article>
  </div>
</Base>
